/** @mainpage Qore ncurses Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section ncursesintro ncurses Module Introduction

    The \c ncurses module provides a modern, thread-safe terminal UI API for %Qore
    based on the system ncurses library. The API focuses on clear, high-level
    primitives rather than exposing the entire low-level curses surface.

    The module has three layers:
    - <b>C++ binary module</b> (\c ncurses): Session, Window, Panel, TestTerminal, and Ansi classes
    - <b>@ref ncursesuiintro "NcursesUi" toolkit</b>: a complete widget tree with layouts, event
      dispatch, and standard components (load with \c %%requires \c NcursesUi)
    - <b>@ref ncursesrepluiintro "NcursesReplUi" helpers</b>: InputBuffer, History, LayoutCalc,
      and ScrollCalc for building REPL-style UIs (load with \c %%requires \c NcursesReplUi)

    This module is released under the LGPL 2.1 license.

    To use the module in a %Qore script, use the \c %%requires directive:
    @code %requires ncurses @endcode

    @section ncurses_quickstart Quick Start

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses

Ncurses::Session s();
Ncurses::Window w = s.screen();

w.clear();
w.drawText(1, 2, "Hello, Ncurses!", {fg: Ncurses::Color::Cyan, attrs: Ncurses::Attr::Bold});
w.refresh();

w.readEvent();
s.close();
    @endcode

    @section ncurses_threads Threading

    All ncurses calls are serialized by a module-level recursive lock. Input
    calls release the lock between polling cycles so other threads can draw
    while one thread waits for input.

    @section ncurses_mouse Mouse Support

    Mouse input is supported through @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::readEvent "Window::readEvent()".
    When enabled (the default), mouse events are reported as input events with
    \c type = \c InputType::Mouse and a \c mouse hash containing coordinates and
    button state.

    @section ncurses_styles Style System

    All drawing methods accept an optional \c style hash with keys from the
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::Style "Style" hashdecl:
    - \c fg: foreground color from @ref Qore::Ncurses::Color "Color" enum (e.g. \c Color::Cyan)
    - \c bg: background color from @ref Qore::Ncurses::Color "Color" enum (e.g. \c Color::Blue)
    - \c attrs: bitwise combination of @ref Qore::Ncurses::Attr "Attr" enum values (e.g. \c Attr::Bold)

    @par Style example:
    @code{.py}
# yellow bold text on a blue background
hash<auto> style = {"fg": Ncurses::Color::Yellow, "bg": Ncurses::Color::Blue, "attrs": Ncurses::Attr::Bold};
w.drawText(0, 0, "Styled text", style);

# cyan text with default background
w.drawText(1, 0, "Cyan text", {"fg": Ncurses::Color::Cyan});
    @endcode

    @section ncurses_ansi ANSI SGR Text

    The module supports rendering text that contains ANSI SGR escape sequences
    (e.g. output from compilers, test runners, or other tools). The
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::Ansi "Ansi" class provides helpers for parsing and
    measuring ANSI text, and the \c drawAnsi* family of Window methods render
    it directly.

    @par ANSI rendering example:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses

Ncurses::Session s();
Ncurses::Window w = s.screen();
w.clear();

string esc = chr(27);
string msg = esc + "[36mCyan title" + esc + "[0m " + esc + "[32mOK" + esc + "[0m";
w.drawAnsiTextInsideLine(0, msg);
w.refresh();

w.readEvent();
s.close();
    @endcode

    @par ANSI measurement:
    @code{.py}
# measure visible width (excludes escape sequences)
int width = Ncurses::Ansi::visibleLength(msg);

# strip ANSI codes entirely
string plain = Ncurses::Ansi::strip(msg);
    @endcode

    @section ncurses_tools Tools

    The module installs a full-screen ncurses REPL frontend backed by the
    %QoreRepl module:
    - \c qrepl — interactive Qore REPL with menus, file browser, output capture, scrollable
      output, and an animated About dialog

    @section ncurses_ui_widgets UI Widget Toolkit (NcursesUi)

    The @ref ncursesuiintro "NcursesUi" module provides a complete terminal UI
    toolkit built on the ncurses binary module. Load it with:
    @code %requires NcursesUi @endcode

    @subsection ncurses_ui_arch Architecture

    The toolkit is organized around a <b>widget tree</b>:
    - @ref NcursesUi::Widget "Widget" is the base class; each widget has a parent, children,
      geometry (@ref NcursesUi::Rect "Rect"), and focus state
    - @ref NcursesUi::BoxLayout "BoxLayout" and @ref NcursesUi::SplitLayout "SplitLayout"
      manage child geometry automatically
    - @ref NcursesUi::Application "Application" owns the Session, root widget, and event loop
    - @ref NcursesUi::EventDispatcher "EventDispatcher" routes keyboard and mouse events
      through the widget tree with a modal stack for popups

    @subsection ncurses_ui_app Application Pattern

    @par Minimal NcursesUi application:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses
%requires NcursesUi

NcursesUi::Application app();
NcursesUi::Widget root();

# add a vertical box layout with two children
NcursesUi::BoxLayout layout(NcursesUi::Orientation::Vertical);
root.addChild(layout);

# add widgets to the layout...
NcursesUi::StatusBarWidget bar();
bar.setSegments(({"text": "Ready", "style": {"fg": Ncurses::Color::Cyan}}));
layout.addChild(bar, NcursesUi::SizePolicy::Fixed);

app.setRoot(root);
app.run();
    @endcode

    @subsection ncurses_ui_panels Panel Widgets and Modal Dialogs

    @ref NcursesUi::PanelWidget "PanelWidget" creates a floating overlay backed by an ncurses
    Panel. @ref NcursesUi::DialogWidget "DialogWidget" extends it with a message and
    configurable buttons. Push a panel as modal via
    @ref NcursesUi::Application "Application::pushModal()" to capture all input until dismissed.

    @subsection ncurses_ui_widgets_list Standard Widget Components

    |!Widget|!Description
    |@ref NcursesUi::ListWidget "ListWidget"|Selectable scrollable list with keyboard and mouse support
    |@ref NcursesUi::TextInputWidget "TextInputWidget"|Single-line text input with cursor and optional history
    |@ref NcursesUi::ScrollableWidget "ScrollableWidget"|Viewport wrapper with scrollbar rendering
    |@ref NcursesUi::MenuBarWidget "MenuBarWidget"|Horizontal menu bar with dropdown support and Alt+key hotkeys
    |@ref NcursesUi::DropdownMenuWidget "DropdownMenuWidget"|Popup menu for MenuBarWidget items
    |@ref NcursesUi::StatusBarWidget "StatusBarWidget"|Status bar with styled left/right-aligned segments
    |@ref NcursesUi::DialogWidget "DialogWidget"|Modal dialog with message and configurable buttons
    |@ref NcursesUi::FileBrowserWidget "FileBrowserWidget"|Split-pane file system browser
    |@ref NcursesUi::ComboBoxWidget "ComboBoxWidget"|Dropdown selector
    |@ref NcursesUi::CheckboxWidget "CheckboxWidget"|Toggle checkbox
    |@ref NcursesUi::RadioGroupWidget "RadioGroupWidget"|Mutually exclusive radio buttons
    |@ref NcursesUi::TableWidget "TableWidget"|Column-based data table

    For full documentation of each widget, see the @ref ncursesuiintro "NcursesUi module documentation".

    @section ncurses_testing Automated Testing with TestTerminal

    @ref Qore::Ncurses::TestTerminal "TestTerminal" creates a pseudo-terminal
    (PTY) that can be passed to
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session "Session" for fully automated, headless tests.
    The @ref NcursesUi::TestHarness "TestHarness" wrapper class from
    @ref ncursesuiintro "NcursesUi" simplifies creating a Session + Application
    pair for widget testing.

    @par TestTerminal example:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses

# create a 24x80 pseudo-terminal
Ncurses::TestTerminal tt(24, 80);

# create a session on the test terminal
Ncurses::Session s({"test_terminal": tt});
Ncurses::Window w = s.screen();

# inject input and verify output
s.injectEvent({"type": Ncurses::InputType::Char, "ch": "x"});
auto ev = w.readEvent(100, 25);
# ev.ch == "x"

# read back screen contents
list<string> rows = s.snapshot();
# rows[0] contains whatever was drawn on row 0

s.close();
    @endcode

    @par TestHarness example:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses
%requires NcursesUi

NcursesUi::TestHarness th(24, 80);

# create and test widgets using th.session() and th.app()
NcursesUi::Widget root();
th.app().run(root, sub () {
    # test code runs here with the event loop active
});

th.close();
    @endcode

    @section ncurses_examples Examples

    The source distribution includes:
    - \c examples/dashboard.qr: lightweight ticking status dashboard demonstrating
      Session, Window, drawText() with styles, and readEvent() polling
    - \c examples/panels.qr: panel stacking and z-order toggling with Panel.top()
    - \c examples/threaded_input.qr: multi-threaded terminal I/O with a background
      reader thread and Mutex synchronization

    @section ncurses_api API Overview

    Helper classes:
    |!Class|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Ncurses::Session "Session"|Owns the terminal state and lifecycle
    |@ref Qore::Ncurses::Window "Window"|Drawing and input on a surface
    |@ref Qore::Ncurses::Panel "Panel"|Layered stacking for windows
    |@ref Qore::Ncurses::TestTerminal "TestTerminal"|PTY-based helper for automated tests

    Drawing helpers:
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::drawTextClipped "Window::drawTextClipped()": clips to window bounds
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::drawTextInside "Window::drawTextInside()": clips to the inner area of a boxed window
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::drawTextInsideLine "Window::drawTextInsideLine()": clears and draws a line inside a box
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::drawAnsiText "Window::drawAnsiText()": draws ANSI SGR colored text
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::drawAnsiTextInside "Window::drawAnsiTextInside()": draws ANSI SGR text inside a box
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::drawAnsiTextInsideLine "Window::drawAnsiTextInsideLine()": clears and draws ANSI SGR text
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::clearInsideLine "Window::clearInsideLine()": clears a line inside a box

    ANSI helpers:
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Ansi "Ansi": parse, wrap, and measure ANSI SGR text

    Refresh helpers:
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::noutrefresh "Window::noutrefresh()": queue a window for refresh
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session::updatePanels "Session::updatePanels()": queue panel updates
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session::doupdate "Session::doupdate()": flush the virtual screen
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::setLeaveOk "Window::setLeaveOk()": keep cursor position in other windows
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::saveCursor "Window::saveCursor()": save the cursor position
    - @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::restoreCursor "Window::restoreCursor()": restore the cursor position
*/
